--------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MIT license above covers VM-Pilot's own code only.

VM-Pilot does not include, bundle, modify, or redistribute any of the
components below. Each is obtained from the PowerShell Gallery at runtime --
onto the host machine, or inside the VM -- directly from its publisher, and
remains subject to its own license. Nothing here is conveyed by VM-Pilot.


OSD  --  https://github.com/OSDeploy/OSD
  David Segura (@OSDeploy).  GPL-3.0.

  The module behind OSDCloud, and the engine behind VM-Pilot's entire
  "get Windows media" story. VM-Pilot calls its Get-FeatureUpdate cmdlet to
  resolve Microsoft's official media download URL, filename and SHA256 for a
  given release, channel and language. Installed on the host, on demand, the
  first time media is downloaded.


HyperV.VMFactory  --  https://github.com/SasStu/HyperV.VMFactory
  Sascha Stumpler.  MIT.

  Creates the Generation 2 / Secure Boot / vTPM virtual machine that VM-Pilot
  boots from the parent VHDX. Installed on the host, on demand, on first VM
  build.


Get-WindowsAutopilotImportGUICommunity
  https://github.com/markorr321/Get-WindowsAutopilotImportCommunity

  The in-VM enrollment GUI used by Online mode. Installed inside the VM from
  the PowerShell Gallery on first run of C:\import.bat. It fronts Andrew
  Taylor's community AutoPilot engine (below).


Get-WindowsAutopilotInfoCommunity
  https://github.com/andrew-s-taylor/WindowsAutopilotInfo
  Andrew Taylor, forked from the original Get-WindowsAutopilotInfo by
  Michael Niehaus.  GPL-3.0.

  VM-Pilot does not invoke or ship this script. It is named here for
  completeness because VMPilotCollect.ps1 writes its v2 device-identifier CSV
  in the same headerless format this script produces with
  `-identifier -OutputFile`, so the two outputs are interchangeable for
  Intune's "Import device identifiers" upload; and because Reset-VMPilot.ps1
  deletes a copy that VM-Pilot 0.5.0 and earlier cached on the host.


Microsoft Windows

  Windows and Windows installation media are the property of Microsoft and
  are governed by Microsoft's own license terms. VM-Pilot neither includes
  nor redistributes any Microsoft software: media is downloaded by the user,
  on the user's machine, directly from Microsoft's delivery network.
